s both conditions, we can use the symmetry and properties of the cross product.\n\n2. **Using the Cross Product Condition**:\n   The vector $\\mathbf{w}$ must be orthogonal to both $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ because $\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{v}$. This means $\\mathbf{w}$ lies in a plane perpendicular to $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$.\n\n3. **Geometric Interpretation**:\n   Given the constraints, $\\mathbf{w}$ can be represented as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$. Without loss of generality, let's assume $\\mathbf{w}$ can be written as:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Computing the Dot Product**:\n   We need to compute $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w})$. Substituting $\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}$, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{v} \\times (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v})).\n   \\]\n\n   Using the distributive property of the cross product, this becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{v}).\n   \\]\n\n   Since $\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{u} = -(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v})$, the condition $\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$, simplifies the expression to:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{u} \\cdot (-(\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v})\n   \\]\n\n   Since $\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$, their dot product is zero:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}) = 0\n   \\]\n\nThus, the value of $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w})$ is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0}\n\\]",
