      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "First, let's calculate the vector $\\mathbf{w}$. Since $\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{w}$, we know that $\\mathbf{w}$ must contain the components of $\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}$. The cross product $\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}$ is orthogonal to both $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ and has a magnitude equal to the sine of the angle between them.\nIf $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are unit vectors, then the magnitude of $\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}$ is $\\sin(\\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is the angle between $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$.\nTherefore, the magnitude of $\\mathbf{w}$ is $\\sin(\\theta) + 1$.\nSince $\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{v}$, the cross product $\\mathbf{v}$ is orthogonal to both $\\mathbf{w}$ and $\\mathbf{u}$.\nIn order for $\\mathbf{v}$ to obtain a component in the $\\mathbf{u}$ direction, one of the unit vectors $u$ or $v$ must form a right angle with $\\mathbf{v}$. Without loss of generality, we assume that $\\theta = 30$ degrees, then $\\sin(30) = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ has the components $\\dfrac{1}{2}(-\\mathbf{u})$ and $-\\dfrac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{u})$. Thus, $\\mathbf{w}$ has the components $\\dfrac{-1}{2}(\\mathbf{u})$ and $-\\dfrac{1}{2}(\\mathbf{u})$.\n\nA | B\n\\boxed{1-\\dfrac{5\\sqrt{3}}{8}}",
