    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "We are given that $\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{w} \\times \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{v}.$  Putting $x=\\mathbf{u}$ and $y=\\mathbf{v},$ we can write this as:\n\\[\\mathbf{z} = \\mathbf{l} \\times \\mathbf{y} + \\mathbf{l}\\]and\n\\[\\mathbf{z} \\times \\mathbf{l} = \\mathbf{y}.\\]Expanding the first equation, we have\n\\[\\mathbf{z} = \\mathbf{y} \\times \\mathbf{l} + \\mathbf{l} \\times \\mathbf{y} + \\mathbf{l}.\\]We can see that $\\mathbf{z} \\times \\mathbf{l} = \\mathbf{y}.$  Hence, $\\mathbf{l}$ is perpendicular to both $\\mathbf{z}$ and $\\mathbf{y},$ which means $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w}) = \\mathbf{v} \\cdot (\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{l}).$  But $\\mathbf{z} = \\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} + \\mathbf{l},$ which means $\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{z} - \\mathbf{l}.$\n\nSetting the two equations for $\\mathbf{u} \\times \\mathbf{v}$ equal, we get\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{y} \\times \\mathbf{l} + \\mathbf{l} \\times \\mathbf{y} + \\mathbf{l} &= \\mathbf{z} - \\mathbf{l}, \\\\\n\\mathbf{y} \\times \\mathbf{l} + \\mathbf{l} \\times \\mathbf{y} + \\mathbf{l} &= \\mathbf{z} - \\mathbf{l} - \\mathbf{l}, \\\\\n\\mathbf{y} \\times \\mathbf{l} + \\mathbf{l} \\times \\mathbf{y} + \\mathbf{l} &= \\mathbf{z} - 2\\mathbf{l}.\n\\end{align*}Then $\\mathbf{y} \\times \\mathbf{l} + \\mathbf{l} \\times \\mathbf{y} + 3\\mathbf{l} = \\mathbf{z},$ which means\n\\[\\mathbf{z} = 3\\mathbf{l}\\]for any solution $\\mathbf{l}.$  Hence, $\\mathbf{u} \\cdot (\\mathbf{v} \\times \\mathbf{w}) = \\mathbf{v} \\cdot \\mathbf{z} = \\mathbf{v} \\cdot 3 \\mathbf{l} = \\boxed{\\tfrac{3}{2}}.$The answer is: 3/2",
